wumpus wrote:
> I appreciate your analysis here.  I'm still left wondering at the
> principles that are being defended by the FSF with the GPL.  The
> overall effect of the GPL seems to me to be to cripple the software it
> is applied to in certain ways, ways that are at least as meaningful,
> and possibly much more so than iOS devices are 'crippled' by the App
> Store's terms.
>
> When the licensing you apply to your software prevents it from being
> used in good faith, you are, as you note above, shooting yourself in
> the foot if your aim is to maximize the ability of people to use it in
> good faith.  But maybe that's not what the FSF is aiming for?

The aim is to ensure that any user of the software will be able to
change it and continue using the changed version, and distribute their
changed version for free if they want to. This is pretty much exactly
contrary to Apple's aims here, and the two end up incompatible as a
result.

I'm glad that NetHack has this sort of license, really (although its
license is a very old and buggy version of the GPL; the most annoying
bug is its complete incompatibility with other licenses even if they
have very similar spirits). I got interested in NetHack, started
writing patches, and now I'm maintaining a variant. And even though the
DevTeam are not putting out code, and thus not useful to anyone any more
for continuing the game, the community can still continue it precisely
because I've got that right to change it.

An iPhone version of NetHack wouldn't allow this. Sure, I'd probably be
able to get hold of the source. But I wouldn't be able to test any
patches I wrote for it, because I wouldn't be able to put them on my
iPhone. And I wouldn't be able to share what I'd done with other people,
even if I did it, due to the App Store's restrictions; Apple don't want
people buying programs and then giving away free copies. (The GPL - and
NetHack's license - do allow you to sell the licensed program, or
a modified copy of it. But they don't allow you to restrict the ability
of anyone you sell it to to make their own copies and give them away, so
realistically you'd only be able to sell one copy of the program itself
before it become freely usable.)

Or to put it another way: if NetHack had originally come out as a game
on the iPhone, none of this ecosystem around it could exist. As soon as
the original developers lost interest in it, it'd be stuck in time at
that point, and nobody could improve it. Even if that had happened to
3.4.3, we'd be stuck in oblivion forever; there'd be no NAO (with its
many bugfixes and interface improvements), no UnNetHack, no NetHack 4.
And most likely, we'd all have been stuck playing Hack 1.0, and likely
all have gotten bored of it by now.

